sections.s2_technical:
The first design decision is how to generate the short key. The cleanest approach is to assign each new URL a unique numeric id from a counter or database sequence, then encode that number in base62 using the sixty-two characters of digits, lowercase, and uppercase letters. Base62 is chosen because it is URL-safe and compact: seven base62 characters cover over three and a half trillion values, more than enough.

```java
// Base62 encode a numeric id into a short key
public String encode(long id) {
    String alphabet = "0123456789abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyzABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ";
    StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
    if (id == 0) return "0";
    while (id > 0) {
        sb.append(alphabet.charAt((int)(id % 62)));
        id /= 62;
    }
    return sb.reverse().toString();
}
```

The second decision is the data model, which is a simple mapping from short key to long URL, plus metadata like creation time, expiry, owner, and a hit counter. Because the id is unique, the base62 key is unique, so there are no collisions by construction, unlike a naive hash approach which must handle collisions.

The third decision is that this is a read-heavy system; a link is created once but clicked thousands of times. So you put a cache like Redis in front of the database. On a redirect, you check the cache first, and only on a miss do you hit the database and then populate the cache. This is the cache-aside pattern, and it is what keeps redirect latency in single-digit milliseconds.

```java
// Cache-aside read on redirect
public String resolve(String shortKey) {
    String url = cache.get(shortKey);         // Redis GET
    if (url == null) {                         // cache miss
        url = repository.findByKey(shortKey);  // DB lookup
        if (url != null) cache.put(shortKey, url);  // populate cache
    }
    return url;
}
```

Senior nuances include custom aliases, where a user requests a specific key like "nbi-salary" and you must check it is not already taken; expiration, where you store a time-to-live and reject or delete expired keys; and analytics, where each redirect increments a counter asynchronously via a message queue so that counting clicks never slows the redirect. You also decide consistency: a brand-new link might not be in every cache immediately, which is fine because eventual consistency on a redirect is acceptable, unlike money.

